# outlier-halo.xyz - a 6m x 6m x 3m dense plot plus a ONE-SIDED halo of far
# strays: the shape a terrestrial scan actually produces. Beams that miss the
# plot carry on and land hundreds of metres away, predominantly on one side (a
# treeline, a building, atmospheric returns), so unlike outlier-extent.xyz --
# whose strays are near-symmetric and largely cancel in the midpoint -- these
# displace the RAW bounding-box centre far off the content. That displacement
# is what drags the default orbit pivot away from the data. Measured on the
# reported scan (RIEGL ScanPos002, 1.13M pts): raw centre (1605, -978) against
# a content centre of (59, 20).
#
# The halo is deliberately well under 1% of the points, matching that scan's
# tail. A fatter tail would be caught by the backend's 1st-99th percentile box
# and the fixture would stop exercising the failure.
# x y z
